WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:02.604
Before asking my coding agent to write

00:02.604 --> 00:04.907
another helper, I ask it to check

00:04.907 --> 00:07.211
the project first. Here I am using

00:07.211 --> 00:09.514
pi with the Deslop connection enabled. I

00:09.514 --> 00:11.818
give it the existing file so the

00:11.818 --> 00:14.121
request is about the code I actually

00:14.121 --> 00:14.450
have.

00:15.250 --> 00:17.634
The agent reads the file and calls

00:17.634 --> 00:20.018
Deslop to look for similar code. This

00:20.018 --> 00:22.401
is a real tool call against the

00:22.401 --> 00:24.785
open project. I can see the request

00:24.785 --> 00:27.168
and the returned result in the conversation,

00:27.168 --> 00:29.552
instead of relying on the agent to

00:29.552 --> 00:29.893
guess.

00:30.693 --> 00:33.249
Deslop returns two matching files, and pi

00:33.249 --> 00:35.806
points me to them. That gives me

00:35.806 --> 00:38.363
an existing implementation to review before I

00:38.363 --> 00:40.920
create another copy. I read the file

00:40.920 --> 00:43.477
names and the reported locations, then check

00:43.477 --> 00:46.034
the source rather than stopping at the

00:46.034 --> 00:46.764
agent's summary.

00:47.564 --> 00:50.004
I open the matching files side by

00:50.004 --> 00:52.445
side in Deslop. Now I can check

00:52.445 --> 00:54.885
the code and its differences myself. The

00:54.885 --> 00:57.325
agent helped me find a possible reuse;

00:57.325 --> 00:59.765
I still decide whether that implementation fits

00:59.765 --> 01:01.857
the change I want to make.
